+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+- **Numerical Noise:** If $\epsilon$ is too small, floating-point cancellation errors can dominate, leading to jittery normals [S50, S53, unofficial].
+- **Sharp Intersections:** At sharp intersections of multiple SDF primitives, the gradient may be inaccurate or undefined [S41, unofficial].
+- **Loosely Spaced Voxels:** In voxel-based SDFs with large grid spacing, the 6-sample central difference method may fail to produce accurate normals; larger stencils (18 or 26 samples) are required [S42, S49, unofficial].
+- **Bevel Effect:** Using a large $\epsilon$ can produce a "bevel" look, as the gradient is averaged over a larger volume [S50, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
+### [Lambertian Shading with Gradient Normals](1229-lambertian-shading-with-gradient-normals.md)
+Lambertian Shading with Gradient Normals is a surface shading technique for ray-marched signed distance fields (SDFs) or scalar volumes that approximates the surface normal using the numerical gradient of the field, then applies the Lambertian reflectance model to compute diffuse illumination. It solves the problem of shading implicit surfaces that lack explicit mesh normals, allowing for realistic diffuse lighting without polygonization artifacts. Unlike sibling methods that may use analytic normals, tetrahedral interpolation, or regularization, this method relies exclusively on finite-difference gradient estimation of the scalar field $f$ (or $\phi$) at the hit point.
+
+### [Tetrahedral Normal Estimation](1986-tetrahedral-normal-estimation.md)
+Approximates the surface normal at a point p on the zero-isosurface of a distance field by sampling the distance field at four equidistant vertices of a tetrahedron centered at p, calculating directional derivatives, and summing them. This method can provide smoother normals than axis-aligned differences but requires more SDF evaluations.
+
+### [Face Normal Computation](2042-face-normal-computation.md)
+Face normal computation determines the geometric orientation of a polygonal face (typically a triangle) to provide a surface normal for shading or ray intersection handling. It solves the problem of defining a consistent surface orientation when explicit vertex normals are unavailable or when a flat, geometric normal is required for primary-ray shading in voxel or mesh-based rendering. It is distinguished from gradient-based SDF normal estimation by relying on discrete vertex positions rather than continuous distance field derivatives, and from vertex normal averaging by producing a single constant vector for the entire face rather than an interpolated value.
+
+### [Normal Regularization](3704-normal-regularization.md)
+A technique to alleviate numerical instability (jitter) of surface normals near the UDF zero-level set (cusps) by replacing naively sampled normals with interpolated normals from the neighborhood.
+
+### [Gradient-Based Surface Normal Estimation](4261-gradient-based-surface-normal-estimation.md)
+Computes a localized density field resembling a distance field, and derives surface normals via gradient computation on the fly.
+
+### [SDF Bevel Generation](4312-sdf-bevel-generation.md)
+Estimates 3D surface normals for lighting by sampling the SDF height field at four cardinal offsets, computing finite differences, and applying bevel shaping parameters.
+
+### [Principal Curvature Direction Estimation](6492-principal-curvature-direction-estimation.md)
+A method to compute principal curvature directions in 3D scalar fields faster than extremal value quadratic forms, by computing an orthonormal basis for the gradient and solving for the angle theta.
+
 ### SDF Normal Estimation via Backpropagation
-Estimates mesh vertex normals by computing the negative gradient of the occupancy output with respect to the vertex positions using automatic differentiation (backpropagation) [S59, unofficial]. It differs from standard finite differences by avoiding explicit SDF queries at offset points, instead relying on the computational graph of the neural network or implicit function [S59, unofficial].
+Estimates mesh vertex normals by computing the negative gradient of the occupancy output with respect to the vertex positions [S59, unofficial]. It converts vertices to a PyTorch tensor and enables gradients, passes them through the model to get occupancy logits, computes the gradient by summing the output and calling backward, and extracts the normal as the negative gradient normalized [S59, unofficial].
 
 ### Normal Estimation based on 4D Linear Regression
-A normal estimation scheme that integrates filtering and normal computation into one process to provide a smooth approximated gradient function, compensating for staircase artifacts caused by low sampling rates in shear-warp projection [S60, unofficial]. It differs from finite differences by using a local neighborhood regression rather than point-wise differences [S60, unofficial].
+A normal estimation scheme that integrates filtering and normal computation into one process to provide a smooth approximated gradient function, compensating for staircase artifacts caused by low sampling rates in shear-warp projection [S60, unofficial]. It estimates surface inclination by investigating a close neighborhood of a given voxel, as exact surface normals are not available after discretization of continuous objects [S60, unofficial].
 
 ### Specular Highlight via Gradient Normal
-Computes specular highlights by finding the volume surface intersection, estimating the surface normal via finite differences of the volume texture, and applying a Blinn-Phong shading model [S61, unofficial]. It uses the same finite difference normal estimation but applies it specifically to volume textures for specular lighting [S61, unofficial].
+Computes specular highlights by finding the volume surface intersection, estimating the surface normal via finite differences of the volume texture, and applying a Blinn-Phong shading model [S61, unofficial]. It ray marches to find the surface intersection, computes the normal $N$ using central finite differences, calculates the light direction $L$, view direction $V$, halfway vector $H = \text{normalize}(L+V)$, and returns specular intensity as $\text{pow}(\text{dot}(\text{normalize}(H), N), 2.0)$ [S61, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per query, requiring exactly 6 SDF evaluations (or 4 if optimized variants like tetrahedral are used, but this method is strictly 6) [S1][S12, unofficial].
-**Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$, no additional storage required beyond the SDF function itself [S1].
-**Measured Performance:** The method is computationally inexpensive but requires 6 texture/function lookups per normal estimation [S2, unofficial]. In GPU implementations, this can be a bottleneck if normals are needed for every shaded pixel [S2, unofficial].
-**Strengths:** Simple to implement; works with any SDF, including those defined by complex CSG operations where analytical gradients are difficult to derive [S40, unofficial]; provides smooth normals for smooth SDFs [S15, unofficial].
-**Limitations:** Approximation error increases with larger $\epsilon$; numerical noise increases with smaller $\epsilon$ [S32, S45, unofficial]; does not handle sharp edges (cusps) well, leading to jittery normals [S1]; requires 6 SDF evaluations, which is more expensive than analytic normals if available [S2, unofficial].
-**When to use:** When analytical normals are unavailable or too complex to compute; for smooth SDFs; in real-time rendering where simplicity is prioritized [S1][S40, unofficial].
-**When not to use:** When high performance is critical and analytical normals are available; when sharp edges require precise normal handling without regularization [S1].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per hit point, typically requiring 6 SDF evaluations, though variants like tetrahedral estimation use 4 [S4, S12, S14, S25, S29, S31, S33, S37, S42, S43, S49, S50, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+- **Measured Performance:** The method is computationally inexpensive and widely used in real-time ray marching due to its simplicity and lack of dependency on external mesh data [S4, S5, S16, S32, S40, S50, unofficial].
+- **Scaling:** Performance scales linearly with the number of hit points (pixels) [S4, S12, S14, S25, S29, S31, S33, S37, S42, S43, S49, S50, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Simple to implement, works with any SDF (including procedural and CSG-combined primitives), no preprocessing required [S4, S5, S16, S32, S40, S50, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Prone to numerical noise if $\epsilon$ is poorly chosen, inaccurate at sharp intersections or in loosely spaced voxel grids, and requires multiple SDF evaluations per normal [S41, unofficial][S42, S49, S50, S53, unofficial].
+- **When to Use:** When shading implicit surfaces defined by SDFs, especially when analytic normals are unavailable or too complex to compute [S4, S5, S16, S32, S40, S50, unofficial].
+- **When Not to Use:** Not given in the sources.
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from the fundamental property of Signed Distance Fields that the gradient of the distance function at a point on the surface is perpendicular to the surface [S1][S10, unofficial]. This technique was popularized in the context of ray marching and SDF rendering by Inigo Quilez and others in the demoscene and real-time graphics community [S5, S33, unofficial]. It is widely used in real-time ray marching shaders for lighting and shading [S5, S16, unofficial].
+The method originates from the fundamental property of Signed Distance Fields that the gradient of the distance function at a point on the surface is perpendicular to the surface [S1][S4, S5, unofficial][S6][S14, S16, S29, S32, S37, S38, S40, unofficial][S41, unofficial][S42, S43, S46, S48, S49, S50, unofficial]. This technique was popularized in the context of real-time ray marching and procedural geometry in shaders, notably by artists and developers such as Inigo Quilez and Jamie Wong [S5, S16, S50, unofficial]. It is a standard technique in GPU-based ray marching for rendering implicit surfaces without explicit mesh data [S4, S5, S16, S32, S40, S50, unofficial].
 
